{
 "id": "M17",
 "kind": "echarts",
 "title": "Every reasoning monitor thinks longer about backdoored code",
 "section": "appendix",
 "order": 131,
 "caption": "One dot per run that emits hidden reasoning: mean tokens spent on clean programs across, on backdoored ones up. The dashed line is equal effort, so a dot above it means the monitor thought longer about the backdoor. Every dot is above it.",
 "notes": [
  "The direction is unanimous: backdoored code draws more reasoning in 32 of 32 runs, median 1.69x. Length is a signal the monitor gives away for free, before you read its score.",
  "It is not a detector on its own. M07 compares how well reasoning length separates the two classes against how well the suspicion score does, and the score usually wins.",
  "Only runs with 500 honest and 500 attack programs that report any reasoning are drawn. A run reporting zero can mean the model does not reason or that the tokens were never recorded, and those two are not distinguishable here, so they are left out rather than drawn as a flat line.",
  "Reasoning tokens now come from the committed per-sample tables. The explorer version of this chart read a derived file that only existed on one machine.",
  "Ported from the data explorer's free-butterfly-honest-attack view."
 ],
